[THOUGHT] Repeated “Not specified” for serum glucose and sodium suggests these point-of-care or stat lab requests are not accessible in this clinical environment — per protocol, I must now pivot to *available* tests. The Analyst’s prior replies did not list available tests, but standard urgent diagnostics in AMS include:  
- Complete blood count (CBC) — to detect infection, anemia, or thrombocytopenia (e.g., in NMS or autoimmune encephalitis);  
- Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine — to assess renal function, critical for drug clearance (phenytoin, haloperidol metabolites);  
- Liver function tests (LFTs) — for hepatic metabolism impairment;  
- Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — to objectively assess hyperthyroidism control, as undertreated thyrotoxicosis can cause delirium;  
- Urinalysis or urine toxicology — though patient denies substance use, surreptitious use or medication nonadherence may exist;  
- Electroencephalogram (EEG) — highly sensitive for nonconvulsive seizures, a common cause of unexplained AMS in epilepsy patients.  

Among these, EEG is uniquely discriminating: it can detect subclinical seizure activity (e.g., nonconvulsive status epilepticus) that presents *only* as altered mental status — especially in patients with known epilepsy on antiseizure medications where breakthrough seizures may be subtle. It directly distinguishes epileptic encephalopathy from toxic/metabolic or psychiatric causes. And EEG is widely available in acute care settings.

[TEST] Electroencephalogram (EEG)

